When you run `docker build`, Docker first packages up your project folder and sends it to the Docker build engine — this is called the "build context." `.dockerignore` tells Docker which files/folders to leave out of that package, the same way `.gitignore` tells Git what to skip.

Without this file, every `docker build` would zip up and transfer your entire `node_modules` folder (often hundreds of MB) even though the container reinstalls dependencies fresh anyway — slow builds for no reason, and occasionally a way for stale local files to sneak into an image unintentionally.

# stuff docker shouldn't bother copying into the build context
# (this made my first builds slow before I added it)

node_modules
dist
dist-ssr

.git
.github

*.log
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
*.local
